meta {
  name: 9g5. Verify Query Strings Survive the Named-API Rewrite
  type: http
  seq: 28
}

get {
  url: {{apisixApiUrl}}/v1/datasets/{{sagaDataSetId}}/{{sagaSlug}}/Things?$top=1
  body: none
  auth: none
}

tests {
  test("Anonymous sub-resource read with a query string succeeds", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("Upstream honoured the query string", function() {
    // The route rewrites the path with a capture group; a rewrite that dropped the query would
    // still return 200 here, so assert the upstream actually applied $top=1 by pinning the page
    // size rather than the status.
    expect(res.body).to.be.an('object');
    expect(res.body.value).to.be.an('array');
    expect(res.body.value.length).to.be.at.most(1);
  });
}

docs {
  **Saga step 9g5 of 18** — Query strings must survive the named-API path rewrite.

  A named-API route rewrites `/v1/datasets/{id}/{slug}/…` onto the upstream's own path using a capture
  group. The query string is carried separately, so a rewrite regression drops filters and paging while
  still answering 200 — the failure is invisible to a status-only assertion.

  This step therefore pins behaviour rather than status: `$top=1` must come back as at most one element.
  It runs anonymously to confirm the open-data grant does not degrade to a "root document only" read.

  **Precondition:** the dataset is open (step 9f) and the anonymous read already succeeded (step 9g).

  **Expected Response:** 200 OK with a SensorThings collection of at most one entry.
}
